    introduction: `Two frameworks every senior DevOps engineer should be able to discuss precisely:

WESTRUM ORGANIZATIONAL TYPOLOGY (Ron Westrum, 2004 paper "A Typology of Organisational Cultures")

Westrum studied aviation safety and healthcare and found three types of organizations distinguished by how they handle information:

Pathological (power-oriented): information is hoarded for personal advantage; messengers are punished; failure is scapegoated; novelty is suppressed; bridging across silos is discouraged. The classic dysfunction: a manager who knows about a coming disaster and doesn\'t tell their boss because telling would reflect badly on them.

Bureaucratic (rule-oriented): information flow follows official channels; departments defend turf; rules trump mission; novelty leads to "not invented here." The classic dysfunction: a process that everyone knows is broken but nobody can change because "that\'s the procedure."

Generative (performance-oriented): information flows freely to where it\'s needed; teams bridge across silos; failure is investigated for systemic learning; mission > position; novelty is encouraged. The classic feature: an engineer who finds a bug in another team\'s code can DM that team and fix it together, regardless of organizational hierarchy.

The Accelerate research (Forsgren, Humble, Kim, 2018) ran statistical analysis on multi-year DORA survey data and found Generative cultures STRONGLY PREDICT software delivery performance — the four DORA metrics all correlate with Westrum scores.

Implication: culture is not a "soft" concern. It\'s the LIMITING FACTOR for software performance. You can\'t out-engineer a Pathological culture.

CALMS FRAMEWORK (Jez Humble + John Willis, ~2010, extended from CAMS by adding Lean)

CALMS is the prescription side — five operational pillars:

C — Culture: shared accountability between dev and ops; no "throwing over the wall." On-call shared; goals shared; metrics shared.

A — Automation: eliminate manual repetition. CI/CD, IaC, configuration management, testing, deployment, monitoring, runbooks. The 50% rule (SRE-style) caps manual toil.

L — Lean: continuous improvement; eliminate waste; reduce batch sizes; surface and remove queues. Adapted from Toyota Production System / Lean manufacturing.

M — Measurement: "what gets measured gets managed" but with a twist — measure outcomes (DORA metrics, customer satisfaction, error budgets), not vanity metrics (lines of code, hours worked).

S — Sharing: knowledge transfer across teams. Communities of practice, internal blogs, documentation, postmortems published broadly, public conference talks.

The predecessor CAMS (Damon Edwards, John Willis, 2010 Velocity Conference) had four pillars; Humble added Lean explicitly because the Lean influence was under-recognized.

Together: Westrum tells you WHERE you are. CALMS tells you WHAT TO DO to move toward Generative.

The interview test: senior DevOps engineers should be able to:
- Place an organization on the Westrum typology with specific evidence
- Map a specific dysfunction to a CALMS pillar
- Explain why culture (Westrum) is upstream of practice (CALMS)
- Cite Accelerate as the empirical foundation`,
